<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#khedive-of-egypt">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khedive of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1867–1914</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Khedive</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khedive was the title of the hereditary viceroys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Ottoman suzerainty, granted by the sultan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Isma'il Pasha</a> in 1867 and used until 1914. The khedives sought to build an African empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, employing Europeans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> and Charles Gordon. After the British occupation of 1882, real power passed to British officials, and in 1914 the title was replaced by sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Khedive of Egypt" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Converted to svg by Daniel78 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt_(1882%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
